Phase 2: Install CLI Tools on the Jenkins Host / Agent
Your Jenkins pipeline executes shell commands (sh) expecting CLI tools pre-installed on the host machine running the Jenkins agent:

1. SonarQube Scanner
Install sonar-scanner and add it to your system PATH:

docker run -d --name sonarqube \
  --hostname sonarqube \
  --network devsecops-net \
  -p 9000:9000 \
  -e SONAR_SEARCH_JAVAADDITIONALOPTS="-Xmx1g -Xms1g" \
  -e SONAR_WEB_JAVAADDITIONALOPTS="-Xmx1g -Xms1g" \
  sonarqube:9.9-community

Install Snyk globally via npm or binary:

Bash
sudo npm install -g snyk
# OR via curl:
# sudo curl --compressed https://static.snyk.io/cli/latest/snyk-linux -o /usr/local/bin/snyk && sudo chmod +x /usr/local/bin/snyk
3. Trivy Security Scanner
Install Aqua Security Trivy:

Bash
sudo apt-get install wget apt-transport-https gnupg lsb-release -y
wget -qO - https://aquasecurity.github.io/trivy-repo/deb/public.key | gpg --dearmor | sudo tee /usr/share/keyrings/trivy.gpg > /dev/null
echo "deb [signed-by=/usr/share/keyrings/trivy.gpg] https://aquasecurity.github.io/trivy-repo/deb $(lsb_release -sc) main" | sudo tee -a /etc/apt/sources.list.d/trivy.list
sudo apt-get update && sudo apt-get install trivy -y
4. Docker & Kubernetes Permissions
Ensure the jenkins user on your server has permission to execute docker and kubectl commands:

Bash
sudo usermod -aG docker jenkins
# Ensure ~/.kube/config or KUBECONFIG exists for the jenkins user so kubectl commands